{
  "image_name": "a3e1b2d85dd6079bcf530f2b41cf99b5.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates how a “shorted” anion-exchange membrane device removes carbon dioxide from air by coupling reactions at a hydrogen-fed anode and an air-exposed cathode. On the left, hydrogen undergoes the hydrogen oxidation reaction, producing electrons and converting bicarbonate or carbonate species into CO2 and water, so the anode side releases a mixture of H2 and CO2. In the center and right, oxygen from air is reduced at the cathode through the oxygen reduction reaction to form hydroxide, which then reacts with CO2 in the incoming air to generate bicarbonate and carbonate. These anions migrate across the membrane toward the anode, while electrons flow internally through the shorted membrane, linking the two half-reactions without an external circuit.\n\nThe overall effect is a drop in CO2 concentration on the cathode side: inlet air at about 400 ppm CO2 becomes outlet air with less than 8 ppm CO2. The insets emphasize the device architecture responsible for this internal shorting. One panel shows a membrane filled with ionomer and carbon particles, and another shows the cathode region as a layered structure with a shorted membrane, an interlayer, and a cathode catalyst layer.",
